libhsakmt (fmm) deferred-free re-map (gfx803, ROCm 10.0): the va-reuse-defer
patch's re-map of parked objects makes libhsakmt's GPUVM aperture
allocator hand out virtual addresses that the kernel still has mapped,
turning every code-object load into `HSA_STATUS_ERROR_OUT_OF_RESOURCES`.

WHY
---
vLLM 10.0 (torch 2.14) SIGSEGVs on its very first kernel launch on gfx803.
Root-caused end to end on the 10.0 line: the torch add kernel is
registered and found (the earlier "HIP functions_ map race" hypothesis
was wrong); GetStatFunc -> program.load() -> setKernels ->
executable_load_agent_code_object fails because the code BO's GPUVM map is
rejected by the kernel with EINVAL (`amdgpu_vm_bo_map`: "va ... conflict
with ...", verified in dmesg), which surfaces as
HSA_STATUS_ERROR_OUT_OF_RESOURCES; GetFunc then returns
hipErrorNoBinaryForGpu and ihipLaunchKernel's fallback
reinterpret_cast's the host stub as a hipFunction_t, whose "map" is the
stub's own code -> SIGSEGV in amd::Kernel::getDeviceKernel.

The colliding kernel mapping belongs to a freed buffer parked on the
va-reuse-defer FIFO. The defer park branch RE-MAPS the object
(_fmm_map_to_gpu) so a lagging reader of a freed buffer keeps hitting a
valid page; that re-established mapping stays in the kernel's GPUVM
interval tree, and the fmm aperture allocator then hands the same VA range
out again -> the kernel rejects the overlap. HSA-side changes only shift
the failure (HSA swaps, AllocateUncached reverts, deferred-FIFO length)
because the leak is the re-map itself. Removing the re-map eliminates the
collision. The freed object still keeps its VA reserved in the aperture
(the defer's whole point), so the only behavioral loss is the stale-reader
tolerance -- which this 10.0 line no longer needs because the
va-reuse-defer-mapping patch's re-map was the thing creating the stale
kernel state in the first place.

Also unmap before free in fmm_do_release: the kernel's
amdgpu_amdkfd_gpuvm_free_memory_of_gpu refuses to free a BO that is
still GPU-mapped (returns EBUSY); with the re-map gone an aged-out object
was never re-mapped, so the unmap is a no-op -- but it is what makes
fmm_do_release correct in isolation (a parked object that arrives mapped
would otherwise leak its kernel mapping and re-trigger the same collision).

Verified on real hardware: with this + d2h-null-dsthost, the full vLLM
10.0 Qwen3.5-2B bench (qwen35_2b_bench_v3.py) completes with EXIT=0
(prefill 311.0 tok/s, decode 30.2 tok/s, 2101-token prompt / 128 decode;
7.14 record for the same bench: 331.7 / 24.4).

Applies AFTER va-reuse-defer.patch (it edits the park branch and the
release path that patch introduced).

WHAT
---
Remove the _fmm_map_to_gpu re-map from __fmm_release's park branch, and
unmap before the BO free in fmm_do_release so an aged-out parked object
can never leave a stale kernel mapping behind.
diff --git a/projects/rocr-runtime/libhsakmt/src/fmm.c b/projects/rocr-runtime/libhsakmt/src/fmm.c
--- a/projects/rocr-runtime/libhsakmt/src/fmm.c
+++ b/projects/rocr-runtime/libhsakmt/src/fmm.c
@@ -1021,6 +1021,12 @@
 		args.handle = e->object->handles[i];
 		if (!args.handle)
 			continue;
+		/* The defer window re-maps the object, so the kernel still
+		 * considers it mapped; free_memory_of_gpu refuses a mapped BO
+		 * with EBUSY, which would leak the mapping and make the next
+		 * allocation at this VA collide. Unmap first, then free.
+		 */
+		hsakmt_ioctl(ctx->fd, AMDKFD_IOC_UNMAP_MEMORY_FROM_GPU, &args);
 		if (hsakmt_ioctl(ctx->fd, AMDKFD_IOC_FREE_MEMORY_OF_GPU,
 				 &args))
 			pr_err("fmm: deferred BO free failed (%d) for obj at %p\n",
@@ -2441,9 +2447,6 @@
 		 * fault. fmm_do_release() tears the mapping down for real when
 		 * the object ages out of the window.
 		 */
-		if (object->mapping_count == 0)
-			_fmm_map_to_gpu(ctx, aperture, object->start,
-					object->size, object, NULL, 0);
 		fmm_defer_release(ctx, aperture, object);
 	}
 
